---
import HomeLayout from "../layouts/HomeLayout.astro";
---

<HomeLayout
  title="mdbase"
  description="Use the same Markdown files in different apps, keep the main copy where you choose, and approve access explicitly."
>
  <canvas id="diagram" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <section id="intro" class="chapter is-active" data-scene="intro">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h1>What is mdbase?</h1>
        <p>
          An mdbase is a folder of Markdown files with enough shared structure
          for applications to understand them. The files remain readable in a
          text editor and can be moved, copied, versioned, or changed with
          ordinary tools.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/spec/">specification</a> defines the common format.
          Libraries provide validation and queries. <a href="/connect/">Connect</a>
          lets an application request access to a particular collection.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="example" class="chapter" data-scene="collection">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h2>A folder is the database.</h2>
        <p>
          This collection contains project records and the definitions that
          describe them. A project application and a text editor can work with
          the same files.
        </p>
        <pre class="file-tree" aria-label="Example mdbase folder"><code>projects/
├── mdbase.yaml
├── _types/
│   ├── project.md
│   └── person.md
├── projects/
│   └── website-redesign.md
└── people/
    └── sam.md</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="meaning" class="chapter" data-scene="structure">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h2>The records are ordinary Markdown.</h2>
        <p>
          Frontmatter holds fields that applications can validate and query.
          The body remains prose.
        </p>
        <pre class="record-example" aria-label="Example project record"><code>---
type: project
title: Website redesign
status: active
owner: "[[people/sam.md]]"
---

The new site should make mdbase
easier to understand.</code></pre>
        <p>
          A type definition stored in the collection explains what these fields
          mean. Conforming tools use the same rules for links, queries,
          validation, and updates.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="storage" class="chapter" data-scene="modes">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h2>A collection can be local or hosted.</h2>
        <p>
          A local collection is an ordinary folder on your computer. The
          open-source libraries and command-line tools can use it without an
          account.
        </p>
        <p>
          A hosted collection keeps its main copy online. It can still have a
          local Markdown mirror for offline work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="permission" class="chapter" data-scene="grant">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h2>Applications ask for access.</h2>
        <p>
          Connect shows which collection an application wants to use and which
          operations it needs. You might allow an application to find, create,
          and edit project records while leaving deletion blocked.
        </p>
        <p>
          The collection authority checks the grant whenever the application
          performs an operation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="after-approval" class="chapter" data-scene="access">
      <div class="chapter__inner">
        <h2>The collection does not belong to the application.</h2>
        <p>
          Access can be narrowed, paused, or revoked. Removing an application
          does not remove the collection, and the files remain usable with
          other conforming tools.
        </p>
        <p>
          See the <a href="/apps/">current applications</a>, read the
          <a href="/sdk/">developer documentation</a>, or learn about the
          optional <a href="/runtime/">runtime profile</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</HomeLayout>
